1
00:00:02,050 --> 00:00:09,407
For 41 years, the mirror in Sam Ferro’s hallway showed him the same face.

2
00:00:09,407 --> 00:00:15,069
On the first of December, it showed him a different one.

3
00:00:15,139 --> 00:00:17,129
SAM: Who… who are you?

4
00:00:17,129 --> 00:00:24,332
The man in the glass didn’t answer. He lifted one finger, and wrote in the steam.

5
00:00:26,102 --> 00:00:31,267
51. Sam was 41.

6
00:00:44,837 --> 00:00:52,011
Kestrel Hill is a town so steep, it needs a railway just to climb itself.

7
00:00:52,011 --> 00:01:02,502
Two little cars hang on one cable. When one goes up, the other comes down. They pass exactly halfway.

8
00:01:02,502 --> 00:01:12,501
Sam kept that railway running. He lived above its engine house, with his daughter, Pip. She was nine.

9
00:01:14,613 --> 00:01:20,846
Sam raised his right hand. The man in the glass raised his.

10
00:01:20,846 --> 00:01:33,662
A mirror doesn’t really swap left and right. It swaps front and back. Your reflection is you, turned to face the other way.

11
00:01:33,662 --> 00:01:45,253
Same scar on the chin. Same chipped mug on the shelf. Only grey, and thinner, and ten years on.

12
00:01:45,988 --> 00:01:51,908
Behind him, the flat was the same flat. And it wasn’t.

13
00:01:51,908 --> 00:02:01,668
A calendar for the year 2036. One chair at the table. One coat on the hooks.

14
00:02:01,668 --> 00:02:07,852
And Pip’s door, shut, with boxes stacked against it.

15
00:02:08,488 --> 00:02:19,502
Every birthday, Sam pencilled Pip’s height on that doorframe. 5. 6. 7. 8. 9.

16
00:02:19,502 --> 00:02:28,504
In the glass, the marks went on. 10. 11. Then a big jump at 12.

17
00:02:28,504 --> 00:02:37,637
Girls grow fastest at about 11 or 12. Around 9 centimetres in a single year.

18
00:02:37,637 --> 00:02:45,023
13. 14. 15. And then the marks stopped.

19
00:02:48,863 --> 00:02:51,963
PIP: Dad? You’re doing the face.

20
00:02:51,963 --> 00:02:53,120
SAM: What face?

21
00:02:53,120 --> 00:02:56,681
PIP: The worried one. With the eyebrows.

22
00:02:56,681 --> 00:03:00,224
Pip came and stood right next to him.

23
00:03:00,224 --> 00:03:05,207
In the glass, there was nobody there.

24
00:03:05,877 --> 00:03:08,726
SAM: Pip. What do you see in the mirror?

25
00:03:08,726 --> 00:03:14,472
PIP: Me. And you, with toothpaste on your chin. Why?

26
00:03:14,472 --> 00:03:20,630
Only Sam could see it. Only Sam needed to.

27
00:03:25,150 --> 00:03:34,469
Fifteen. Something was going to happen to Pip when she was fifteen. And Sam was going to stop it.

28
00:03:35,875 --> 00:03:39,344
SAM: Not the lake. The ice is too thin.

29
00:03:39,344 --> 00:03:44,513
PIP: It’s really thick, Dad! Everybody’s on it!

30
00:03:45,050 --> 00:03:47,679
SAM: Not the sled run, either.

31
00:03:48,800 --> 00:03:53,179
PIP: Can I come up to the big wheel with you? Just once?

32
00:03:53,179 --> 00:03:57,353
SAM: No. The engine house isn’t for you.

33
00:03:59,113 --> 00:04:02,661
ODILE: You can’t keep a child in a jar, love.

34
00:04:02,661 --> 00:04:04,415
SAM: Watch me.

35
00:04:05,675 --> 00:04:10,472
Every morning, Sam checked the doorframe in the glass.

36
00:04:10,472 --> 00:04:19,030
Every morning, the marks stopped sooner. 14. 13. 12.

37
00:04:19,030 --> 00:04:25,684
The tighter he held on, the faster she was disappearing.

38
00:04:27,837 --> 00:04:37,600
PIP: Dad, look. I drew the big wheel. I counted the teeth through the window. It’s got 120…

39
00:04:37,600 --> 00:04:41,065
SAM: Bed, Pip. Now.

40
00:04:41,065 --> 00:04:43,135
PIP: You never even look.

41
00:04:43,135 --> 00:04:49,711
She folded the drawing in half. And then in half again.

42
00:04:55,150 --> 00:05:02,141
On the twentieth morning, the man in the glass was holding a letter flat against the glass.

43
00:05:02,141 --> 00:05:13,991
It was written backwards. Leonardo da Vinci filled his notebooks with writing like this. To read it, you hold it up to a mirror.

44
00:05:13,991 --> 00:05:23,022
So Sam held up his shaving mirror, and read his daughter’s handwriting, from ten years away.

45
00:05:36,392 --> 00:05:40,483
She hadn’t been lost. She had left.

46
00:05:40,483 --> 00:05:48,339
Not because of the lake. Not because of the sled run. Because of him.

47
00:05:50,537 --> 00:05:58,236
For the first time, Sam and the man in the glass looked at each other the same way.

48
00:06:02,188 --> 00:06:08,267
ODILE: There’s a storm coming over the ridge tonight, Samuel. A big one.

49
00:06:08,267 --> 00:06:11,743
SAM: Listen, Pip. About your drawing…

50
00:06:11,743 --> 00:06:12,801
PIP: Forget it.

51
00:06:12,801 --> 00:06:18,200
SAM: I’ve got the night check. We’ll talk after. I promise.

52
00:06:18,200 --> 00:06:21,002
PIP: You always say after.

53
00:06:27,025 --> 00:06:33,523
The last car of the night left the bottom station at nine o’clock.

54
00:06:34,493 --> 00:06:44,208
ODILE: Sam! Pip’s on the last car. She’s coming up to you. She’s got some drawing for you.

55
00:06:52,688 --> 00:06:57,745
At six minutes past nine, the storm took the power.

56
00:06:57,745 --> 00:07:06,360
Both cars stopped where they always meet. Halfway up the hill. In the dark.

57
00:07:07,550 --> 00:07:10,512
SAM: Pip? Pip, can you hear me?

58
00:07:10,512 --> 00:07:16,075
PIP: Dad? It’s really dark. It’s swinging.

59
00:07:16,825 --> 00:07:23,676
SAM: I’m coming down the steps. Keep talking to me. Tell me about the drawing.

60
00:07:25,562 --> 00:07:33,471
PIP: It’s the big wheel. It’s got 120 teeth. I counted them when you weren’t looking.

61
00:07:33,471 --> 00:07:38,495
SAM: 120. Twenty years on that wheel, and I never knew that.

62
00:07:38,495 --> 00:07:44,101
The lamp was getting weaker. The steps were getting longer.

63
00:07:44,101 --> 00:07:48,003
SAM: Pip. What do you want? Really.

64
00:07:48,003 --> 00:07:56,486
PIP: I want to know how it works. The railway. I want you to show me. Don't keep me out.

65
00:07:56,486 --> 00:08:03,899
It was the first time he had asked. And the first time he had listened to the answer.

66
00:08:05,875 --> 00:08:13,731
Her door was frozen shut. The empty car beside hers was as close as he could get.

67
00:08:14,701 --> 00:08:23,286
SAM: Okay. Lesson one. These two cars are tied to the same cable. Neither one can move alone.

68
00:08:23,286 --> 00:08:29,635
SAM: If we both let our brakes off, the heavier car goes down. And it pulls the lighter one up.

69
00:08:29,635 --> 00:08:30,894
PIP: You’re heavier.

70
00:08:30,894 --> 00:08:38,474
SAM: Much heavier. So you go up, to the engine house, where the stove is. And I go down.

71
00:08:38,474 --> 00:08:43,282
PIP: That’s not fair. You’ll be at the bottom, on your own.

72
00:08:43,282 --> 00:08:47,934
SAM: That’s how it works. One goes up because the other goes down.

73
00:08:47,934 --> 00:08:58,634
Funiculars have worked this way since the 1800s. The motor barely lifts a thing. The weight of one car lifts the other.

74
00:08:58,634 --> 00:09:08,221
SAM: Red lever. Both hands. On three. One. Two. Three.

75
00:09:11,625 --> 00:09:18,433
And that was how Sam Ferro learned the hardest thing a parent ever has to learn.

76
00:09:18,433 --> 00:09:25,740
Not how to hold on. How to let go. Slowly.

77
00:09:34,837 --> 00:09:39,703
PIP: Dad? I made it. I’m by the big wheel.

78
00:09:39,703 --> 00:09:42,515
SAM: Good. Don’t touch anything.

79
00:09:42,515 --> 00:09:44,247
PIP: Too late.

80
00:09:49,525 --> 00:09:54,348
The next morning, Sam almost didn’t look.

81
00:09:54,348 --> 00:10:00,895
The man in the glass was still grey. He still had the chipped mug.

82
00:10:00,895 --> 00:10:09,114
But the marks on the doorframe went all the way up. 16. 17. 19.

83
00:10:09,114 --> 00:10:16,862
And beside him stood a young woman in railway overalls, with a tool bag on her shoulder.

84
00:10:16,862 --> 00:10:21,554
She was still leaving. That part never changed.

85
00:10:21,554 --> 00:10:25,936
But now, she was coming back.

86
00:10:26,706 --> 00:10:29,348
PIP: Dad. Lesson two?

87
00:10:29,348 --> 00:10:33,074
SAM: Lesson two. Get your coat.

88
00:10:37,275 --> 00:10:47,322
The mirror never showed Sam his future. It showed him the man he’d become if he stayed exactly as he was.

89
00:10:47,322 --> 00:10:55,753
Ten years from now, you’ll be somebody you haven’t met yet. You still get to choose who.
